2186045436] [17592186050578 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428661 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050578 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428662 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050578 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428663 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050578 #edb/ref 1031 "The Gram-Schmidt Process for Two Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050578 #edb/ref 1033 2127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050578 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5558fd2c-f6e0-5362-bf8b-7d3500df79a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186160886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186160886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186160886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186160886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186160886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186160886 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186160886 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186160886 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186160886 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7b62c2ac-065d-54c9-abdd-bbf6c6966cb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161780 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161780 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1d/1d054c7daaaeefd4cdf820d49d04d6a0bd544f09e356b859b6291ff7d4588e19.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the third column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161780 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "054b9992-0bf6-5dcc-a04b-038ad6559959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161780 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=0,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=4.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_3=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector \n\n![](images/98/980726b042df523d4c12352fcde141e8c39701a23d72c2815165517dc8ab6949.svg)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3 \\| = \\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/ca/caa7100961d28805820530d11708ac6dee747a75b858494d84fcd4c6e5c5d1fb.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161781 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161781 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/da/dacf2240e98ef232fd9b5cd8d12baddb808e6f30c2956d6545370095346c9f22.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the first and second columns of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161781 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6714653-0cfa-5049-9ae9-f921d46d03bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161781 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1,$ $\\lambda_2=-1,$ and $\\lambda_3=8.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/b9/b90c25fdfb269ffd1c6fe30dc77f9142986f1b72adc23b5e71e2d9a4182795ab.svg) and ![](images/f1/f1313b31d2bce0d0358f5a9255aeb04f13cecfc0d65ae1d1028024ee5a029bc8.svg)\n\nNotice that eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n![](images/bf/bf1b9a7dbbca5e1df2e7acb7cf62ef04e35791252bbd8020715c4d7bde23c577.svg)\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{5}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{e}_1$ and $\\mathbf{e}_2$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161782 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8175" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161782 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a2/a21020be45202ebc19fdaeb8d3c334775f072ca037d68805e635e824966d7fd2.svg)\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/86/860c02625e5bbb5ac0fcb54e0895274ea38d0462d810da582f75351cbe094b36.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161782 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4779b6be-97c9-5389-b25e-e8f438f0a7fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161782 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1039 "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161988 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1039 "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161989 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161990 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162215 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162215 #edb/ref 1045 "Diagonalization of 3x3 Symmetric Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162215 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162215 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162215 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162215 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a251e410-6f92-5d37-91f6-3fe7f0377f20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162598 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162598 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162598 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162598 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162598 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2f119be-4dbb-5583-8e23-114609166369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162598 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162599 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162599 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93727" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162599 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162599 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "474fce27-4b23-5be8-8b2d-389558be5e18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162599 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ Computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162624 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162624 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162624 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162624 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dec4c6f4-86dd-50ba-b4f8-5dac075a34ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162624 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162625 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162625 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162625 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162625 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162625 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d443cf2-e450-59f7-912e-8007eb24ac35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162625 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are orthogonal. Finally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162848 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162848 #edb/ref 1061 30532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162848 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a486be1-0f5d-50ea-a245-3aedf2c597b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162848 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162870 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162870 #edb/ref 1061 30594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162870 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34cd3b8c-2df0-5886-a6f1-17125ede8748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162870 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162871 #edb/ref 1061 30595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162871 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9df74bd-9e6b-5113-a23f-86c30ccc850b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162871 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162872 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162872 #edb/ref 1061 30597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162872 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9795272-744c-5dd3-afc9-73a980ee4bf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162872 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162915 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162915 #edb/ref 1061 31356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162915 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "63db5c7a-ccd4-5948-af8b-421d4a60d2da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162915 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163223 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a real matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nThe procedure for finding an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ is similar to diagonalizing a regular (diagonalizable) $3\\times 3$ matrix:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of *unit orthogonal* eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nAs an example, consider the matrix \n![](images/ac/acba5373e2f2470930adec855df9958508d9f41aba5d3fc6988b0001c07a9dfd.svg)\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. So let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\qquad \\lambda_2=-6, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-9.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** We need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n![](images/cd/cddf44571b3aba21dca82cc12664733469c17f30ed3a9a9cfc62acb12230caef.svg)\nNotice that the eigenvectors are mutually orthogonal. \nNow, an orthogonal diagonalization requires each eigenvector to be normalized. So, dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the following unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n![](images/e4/e4bfc5b51cea64bd5cb8d340fbeab1a126aaa3f48ae42b991c2b5af2a25480b3.svg)\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/4b/4b4a8c98b7c4688fab231510b6b1d615f47bed070310d39f3e812cd9fb811395.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** In this example, we automatically get orthogonal eigenvectors because $A$ has three *distinct* eigenvalues. However, in the case of repeated eigenvalues, the corresponding eigenvectors might not be orthogonal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163223 #edb/ref 1065 4881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163223 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0ff932ad-00a4-58cd-952f-55c21e02ef43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163223 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's recall the algorithm for diagonalizing a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A.$\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of unit orthogonal eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNote the following important points regarding step 2 of this process:\n\n- If $A$ has mutually distinct eigenvalues, the eigenvectors will automatically be orthogonal, so we only need to normalize them.\n\n- If $A$ has *repeated* eigenvalues, then the eigenvectors corresponding to these repeated eigenvalues *may not* be orthogonal. Therefore, we have to apply the Gram-Schmidt process to obtain orthogonal vectors.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/28/2807ddab01a673d01c385b0dcc1b77d007896f803376006e7f81f639930fecdc.svg)\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. Let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n![](images/95/95d0808a376ad679118ee26b9387cc3037862b74f12945db886a2ffd7738d525.svg)\nNotice that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n![](images/b4/b4aaec2c201e4ae0aff1054c57fdf4cc860dac1fa6bd38a9b58865b5306b8ce2.svg)\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ form an *orthogonal* basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n![](images/c9/c9a112d21b6868b583f521a73a5aa491a40ce46c365adf6ddc3f7eeb60c9f5fe.svg)\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/f1/f111684678ff06708513e20f22db25b4d6246dda2a8ce5f50b5905c9a13b0cbd.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1065 5013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ad655c9-a00e-5174-a98f-6ae8ab07843d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1067 "Symmetric Matrices With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354773 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354773 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354773 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c067b7a-e3e8-51e6-a73a-b7f6dca44c63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354887 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354887 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354887 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18f19226-ff02-5443-a9f8-cc41f721eaa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354896 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354896 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354896 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a9b3586-3f9f-5bd3-ad90-e25a93828ae7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354964 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354964 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1759218