f 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357249 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357249 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186356205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357249 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b7490596-2fa1-53bc-8ada-e942488fb1b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357308 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186354997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357308 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357308 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357308 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357308 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357308 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357308 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357308 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186356505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357308 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dad9f5dc-8923-584b-b5c4-0c96615d0676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357705 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357705 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357705 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357705 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\,∗\\, \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357705 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c497ac69-8d8c-5ea0-b36e-659d4cac4441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357705 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357706 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357706 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357706 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357706 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\, & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\, & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\,∗\\, & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357706 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa2a5fda-40ff-59dc-b588-ee085fb956e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357706 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357732 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357732 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94351" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357732 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357732 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second and third columns of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357732 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fecab0a-cd00-5e03-af2a-81daa57c4cbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357732 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{2}={v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & ={v}_{3}-\\frac{{v}_{3}\\cdot {e}_{2}}{{e}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{2}}{e}_{2} \\\\  & ={v}_{3}-\\frac{1}{5}{e}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{2}$ and ${e}_{3}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the second and third columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{3}}{‖{e}_{3}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{24}{5}}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364530 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364530 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364530 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364530 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc0d6469-4523-5a6a-b241-3d642027e56c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364530 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364531 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364531 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364531 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364531 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8040c4b6-73ba-50cc-80d0-f343c5c817c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364532 #edb/ref 1080 "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364532 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364532 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "33d33072-0e95-532d-bfe9-f8a1d3590433" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364533 #edb/ref 1080 "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364533 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1759218